WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.662
A card account can be viewed

2
00:00:01.662 --> 00:00:03.540
at a terminal and updated later

3
00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:04.407
by a batch job.

4
00:00:04.407 --> 00:00:06.557
CardDemo puts both worlds in one

5
00:00:06.557 --> 00:00:07.433
repository.

6
00:00:07.433 --> 00:00:09.925
Its core is COBOL running under

7
00:00:09.925 --> 00:00:12.417
CICS, with VSAM records and JCL

8
00:00:12.417 --> 00:00:14.046
driven processing.

9
00:00:14.046 --> 00:00:16.589
That combination makes the project useful

10
00:00:16.589 --> 00:00:18.989
for studying mainframe behavior before

11
00:00:18.989 --> 00:00:19.766
changing it.

12
00:00:19.766 --> 00:00:22.300
This is demonstration software, not a

13
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.360
production banking platform.

14
00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:26.661
The most useful starting question is

15
00:00:26.661 --> 00:00:28.889
simple: which program owns the next

16
00:00:28.889 --> 00:00:30.745
step, and which record does it

17
00:00:30.745 --> 00:00:31.264
change?

18
00:00:31.264 --> 00:00:33.246
First, look at the work users

19
00:00:33.246 --> 00:00:34.154
can perform.

20
00:00:34.154 --> 00:00:36.261
The regular menu leads to account

21
00:00:36.261 --> 00:00:38.969
and card screens, transaction workflows,

22
00:00:38.969 --> 00:00:40.850
and billing related actions.

23
00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:43.141
A separate administrator menu manages

24
00:00:43.141 --> 00:00:45.710
users and optional reference maintenance.

25
00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:48.166
These are terminal workflows, not web

26
00:00:48.166 --> 00:00:48.933
endpoints.

27
00:00:48.933 --> 00:00:50.974
In the source tree, programs live

28
00:00:50.974 --> 00:00:53.524
beside map definitions and shared record

29
00:00:53.524 --> 00:00:54.107
layouts.

30
00:00:54.107 --> 00:00:56.947
The base application contains twenty nine

31
00:00:56.947 --> 00:00:58.051
COBOL programs.

32
00:00:58.051 --> 00:01:00.562
The optional integration directories add

33
00:01:00.562 --> 00:01:02.934
capabilities, but they are not required

34
00:01:02.934 --> 00:01:04.259
for every core screen.

35
00:01:04.259 --> 00:01:07.050
Next, follow signon into the menu.

36
00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:09.607
The initial transaction enters the signon

37
00:01:09.607 --> 00:01:12.164
program, which distinguishes first entry

38
00:01:12.164 --> 00:01:14.010
from a returning interaction.

39
00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:16.039
It reads the application user record

40
00:01:16.039 --> 00:01:17.675
and routes according to role.

41
00:01:17.675 --> 00:01:19.440
Sending a screen does not keep

42
00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.205
one program waiting forever.

43
00:01:21.205 --> 00:01:23.306
A CICS return arranges the next

44
00:01:23.306 --> 00:01:26.215
transaction and carries context forward.

45
00:01:26.215 --> 00:01:28.787
The shared communication area preserves

46
00:01:28.787 --> 00:01:31.286
navigation and account context between

47
00:01:31.286 --> 00:01:31.727
tasks.

48
00:01:31.727 --> 00:01:33.907
This pseudo conversational pattern is

49
00:01:33.907 --> 00:01:36.352
central to understanding where validation

50
00:01:36.352 --> 00:01:37.342
and state belong.

51
00:01:37.342 --> 00:01:39.470
A screen change crosses more than

52
00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:40.078
one file.

53
00:01:40.078 --> 00:01:42.652
The COBOL program supplies behavior.

54
00:01:42.652 --> 00:01:45.782
The BMS map defines terminal positions,

55
00:01:45.782 --> 00:01:48.083
attributes, and field sizes.

56
00:01:48.083 --> 00:01:50.309
A symbolic map provides the fields

57
00:01:50.309 --> 00:01:51.921
consumed by the program.

58
00:01:51.921 --> 00:01:54.387
The account viewer map explicitly targets

59
00:01:54.387 --> 00:01:56.305
a twenty four by eighty terminal.

60
00:01:56.305 --> 00:01:58.628
Keep all three representations aligned

61
00:01:58.628 --> 00:02:00.677
when adding input or changing field

62
00:02:00.677 --> 00:02:01.223
lengths.

63
00:02:01.223 --> 00:02:02.733
The view on screen here is

64
00:02:02.733 --> 00:02:04.817
a source grounded schematic, not a

65
00:02:04.817 --> 00:02:06.758
captured application session.

66
00:02:06.758 --> 00:02:08.552
No live host was exercised for

67
00:02:08.552 --> 00:02:09.700
this walkthrough.

68
00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:11.931
The account copybook defines a three

69
00:02:11.931 --> 00:02:13.226
hundred byte record.

70
00:02:13.226 --> 00:02:15.289
Its account key is eleven digits.

71
00:02:15.289 --> 00:02:17.445
Monetary fields use signed decimal

72
00:02:17.445 --> 00:02:19.673
display definitions with an implied

73
00:02:19.673 --> 00:02:20.608
decimal point.

74
00:02:20.608 --> 00:02:22.875
These are byte level contracts, not

75
00:02:22.875 --> 00:02:25.293
interchangeable strings or database

76
00:02:25.293 --> 00:02:25.898
objects.

77
00:02:25.898 --> 00:02:27.783
Online code refers to a CICS

78
00:02:27.783 --> 00:02:29.751
file name, while batch uses a

79
00:02:29.751 --> 00:02:32.456
different file binding for the related

80
00:02:32.456 --> 00:02:33.112
dataset.

81
00:02:33.112 --> 00:02:35.172
A safe change follows those names

82
00:02:35.172 --> 00:02:37.453
across resource definitions and job

83
00:02:37.453 --> 00:02:38.041
control.

84
00:02:38.041 --> 00:02:40.767
Padding, encoding, signs, and record

85
00:02:40.767 --> 00:02:43.494
lengths must survive the entire path.

86
00:02:43.494 --> 00:02:45.666
Batch is the other execution world.

87
00:02:45.666 --> 00:02:48.715
The posting job binds daily transactions,

88
00:02:48.715 --> 00:02:51.340
account data, cross references, and

89
00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:54.219
category balances to its COBOL program.

90
00:02:54.219 --> 00:02:56.549
It also defines rejected output.

91
00:02:56.549 --> 00:02:58.613
Interest calculation and statement

92
00:02:58.613 --> 00:03:01.142
generation are separate responsibilities.

93
00:03:01.142 --> 00:03:03.543
Because online and batch share files,

94
00:03:03.543 --> 00:03:06.020
the close and reopen boundary matters.

95
00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:08.971
This diagram describes relationships, not

96
00:03:08.971 --> 00:03:11.124
a safe job submission sequence.

97
00:03:11.124 --> 00:03:13.545
Initialization can replace data, and

98
00:03:13.545 --> 00:03:16.042
replaying posting can change results.

99
00:03:16.042 --> 00:03:18.184
Start with an isolated snapshot and

100
00:03:18.184 --> 00:03:20.327
inspect each job before running it.

101
00:03:20.327 --> 00:03:22.678
The authorization extension introduces a

102
00:03:22.678 --> 00:03:24.572
different integration boundary.

103
00:03:24.572 --> 00:03:26.900
An MQ request reaches a processor

104
00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:29.478
that reads core VSAM reference data.

105
00:03:29.478 --> 00:03:31.858
Pending authorization information is

106
00:03:31.858 --> 00:03:32.724
stored in IMS.

107
00:03:32.724 --> 00:03:35.331
Separate CICS review screens can mark

108
00:03:35.331 --> 00:03:37.450
fraud through linked Db2 work.

109
00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:39.483
The external point of sale client

110
00:03:39.483 --> 00:03:40.500
is not included.

111
00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:42.829
One important caveat is visible in

112
00:03:42.829 --> 00:03:45.079
the source: the processor uses MQ

113
00:03:45.079 --> 00:03:47.167
operations without syncpoint

114
00:03:47.167 --> 00:03:50.140
participation and replies before updating

115
00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:50.461
IMS.

116
00:03:50.461 --> 00:03:52.304
Do not mistake this flow for

117
00:03:52.304 --> 00:03:55.109
an atomic, exactly once payment service.

118
00:03:55.109 --> 00:03:57.734
The transaction type extension uses Db2

119
00:03:57.734 --> 00:03:59.819
for reference administration.

120
00:03:59.819 --> 00:04:01.268
It does not move the whole

121
00:04:01.268 --> 00:04:03.200
account system into a relational

122
00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:03.821
database.

123
00:04:03.821 --> 00:04:05.934
Online and batch maintenance update

124
00:04:05.934 --> 00:04:07.842
transaction type descriptions.

125
00:04:07.842 --> 00:04:10.263
An extraction job writes sequential

126
00:04:10.263 --> 00:04:12.762
snapshots, and separate loading jobs

127
00:04:12.762 --> 00:04:15.340
populate the VSAM reference files used

128
00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:16.277
by reporting.

129
00:04:16.277 --> 00:04:18.328
That separation is easy to miss.

130
00:04:18.328 --> 00:04:20.264
A successful Db2 edit does not

131
00:04:20.264 --> 00:04:22.820
mean every report immediately sees the

132
00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:23.362
change.

133
00:04:23.362 --> 00:04:25.977
Treat the schema, host declarations,

134
00:04:25.977 --> 00:04:28.920
extracted lengths, and downstream load as

135
00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:30.800
one coordinated contract.

136
00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:33.438
Another optional directory contains date

137
00:04:33.438 --> 00:04:35.416
and account responders over MQ.

138
00:04:35.416 --> 00:04:37.226
These are not the authorization

139
00:04:37.226 --> 00:04:37.897
processor.

140
00:04:37.897 --> 00:04:39.881
The account request starts with a

141
00:04:39.881 --> 00:04:42.290
four character operation followed by an

142
00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:43.849
eleven digit account key.

143
00:04:43.849 --> 00:04:45.917
Replies are padded to one thousand

144
00:04:45.917 --> 00:04:47.342
bytes and sent to a fixed

145
00:04:47.342 --> 00:04:48.198
destination.

146
00:04:48.198 --> 00:04:49.793
The saved reply to queue is

147
00:04:49.793 --> 00:04:51.097
not used for routing.

148
00:04:51.097 --> 00:04:53.886
Some README examples describe a different

149
00:04:53.886 --> 00:04:55.977
protocol, so use the programs as

150
00:04:55.977 --> 00:04:56.984
the authority.

151
00:04:56.984 --> 00:04:59.577
A consumer must preserve byte offsets,

152
00:04:59.577 --> 00:05:01.698
encoding, and the signed amount

153
00:05:01.698 --> 00:05:02.877
representation.

154
00:05:02.877 --> 00:05:05.121
Running this repository requires a

155
00:05:05.121 --> 00:05:07.740
configured host or compatible mainframe

156
00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:08.339
runtime.

157
00:05:08.339 --> 00:05:10.544
There is no universal laptop server

158
00:05:10.544 --> 00:05:11.133
command.

159
00:05:11.133 --> 00:05:13.800
Source transfer, compilation, resource

160
00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.392
installation, and data initialization

161
00:05:16.392 --> 00:05:18.450
each have environment specific

162
00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:19.365
assumptions.

163
00:05:19.365 --> 00:05:21.594
Begin with read only inquiry before

164
00:05:21.594 --> 00:05:22.709
testing updates.

165
00:05:22.709 --> 00:05:25.749
For batch, verify return codes, rejects,

166
00:05:25.749 --> 00:05:28.094
and resulting records against a

167
00:05:28.094 --> 00:05:29.918
reproducible snapshot.

168
00:05:29.918 --> 00:05:32.245
No general automated assertion suite or

169
00:05:32.245 --> 00:05:34.298
checked in continuous integration

170
00:05:34.298 --> 00:05:35.461
workflow was found.

171
00:05:35.461 --> 00:05:37.516
Sample data and utility jobs are

172
00:05:37.516 --> 00:05:39.495
useful inputs, but they are not

173
00:05:39.495 --> 00:05:40.941
proof of correctness.

174
00:05:40.941 --> 00:05:43.958
Across the full repository, the inventory

175
00:05:43.958 --> 00:05:46.640
includes forty two COBOL programs and

176
00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:49.573
sixty one copybooks, including symbolic

177
00:05:49.573 --> 00:05:49.992
maps.

178
00:05:49.992 --> 00:05:52.231
Those counts show why shared contracts

179
00:05:52.231 --> 00:05:54.539
deserve attention before translation.

180
00:05:54.539 --> 00:05:57.312
The repository also contains a Factory

181
00:05:57.312 --> 00:06:00.085
conversion skill, Java templates, and

182
00:06:00.085 --> 00:06:01.514
analysis guidance.

183
00:06:01.514 --> 00:06:03.803
Those Java files are examples, not

184
00:06:03.803 --> 00:06:06.093
a built replacement application.

185
00:06:06.093 --> 00:06:08.558
Packaged runtime archives are samples,

186
00:06:08.558 --> 00:06:10.733
not infrastructure provisioning.

187
00:06:10.733 --> 00:06:13.252
A useful modernization slice preserves

188
00:06:13.252 --> 00:06:15.474
one observable journey, its record

189
00:06:15.474 --> 00:06:17.770
semantics, and its failure behavior

190
00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:19.622
before broadening the scope.

191
00:06:19.622 --> 00:06:21.705
You can remember CardDemo as three

192
00:06:21.705 --> 00:06:22.782
connected paths.

193
00:06:22.782 --> 00:06:24.652
A screen leads through a program

194
00:06:24.652 --> 00:06:25.761
to a shared record.

195
00:06:25.761 --> 00:06:27.623
A batch job binds programs to

196
00:06:27.623 --> 00:06:30.028
datasets and changes the same state.

197
00:06:30.028 --> 00:06:32.144
An optional message path introduces

198
00:06:32.144 --> 00:06:34.055
additional storage and recovery

199
00:06:34.055 --> 00:06:34.806
boundaries.

200
00:06:34.806 --> 00:06:36.678
For your first change, pick one

201
00:06:36.678 --> 00:06:38.838
journey and trace every contract it

202
00:06:38.838 --> 00:06:39.414
touches.

203
00:06:39.414 --> 00:06:42.342
Keep demonstration data isolated, review

204
00:06:42.342 --> 00:06:44.945
host security separately, and verify

205
00:06:44.945 --> 00:06:47.304
behavior on the intended runtime.

206
00:06:47.304 --> 00:06:49.767
Understanding those boundaries is the

207
00:06:49.767 --> 00:06:52.155
foundation for a credible migration,

208
00:06:52.155 --> 00:06:54.543
refactoring, or service extraction.
